  has zero dependencies. The [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m files use CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m but they're a separate Node entry point — not part of
  the browser app.

  That matters, because "other forms will need it later" means a shared session module, and this repo has no module
  system on the browser side to hang one on.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the browser code shares state between files. This is the choice that's most expensive to revisit — every
  file we add afterward inherits it.

  - [1mClassic script + global namespace[0m ([38;5;153mwindow.Session[39m, a new [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m) — matches the existing code
    exactly, no build step, still works when you double-click [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. Costs you the usual globals problems:
    load-order coupling, no encapsulation, awkward to unit test.
  - [1mNative ES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m) — real encapsulation and clean testability, still no build
    step. But module scripts are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so you'd need to serve the page over a local HTTP server from
    then on. That's the one real behavior change.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m (Vite or similar) — best long-run ergonomics, but it introduces a toolchain and a dependency tree into
    a repo that currently has neither. YAGNI for a login form.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m native ES modules. Encapsulation is the thing you actually need for shared session state that other
  forms read, it keeps the repo dependency-free, and the [38;5;153mfile://[39m limitation is a one-time "run a local server" cost
  rather than an ongoing one. If you routinely open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m straight off disk, that flips me to the globals option.
